To dynamically modify configurations and log levels at runtime without 
restarting your Spring Boot application server, you must use specific Spring Boot Actuator and 
Spring Cloud features.

By default, standard Spring properties are immutable after initialization. 
However, you can make them dynamic by combining Spring Boot Actuator with Spring Cloud Commons / Config.

1. How to Change Log Levels at RuntimeYou can modify log levels instantly using Actuator’s built-in 
/actuator/loggers endpoint without adding any extra dependencies.

Step A: Expose the Loggers EndpointAdd the following line to your application.properties or application.yml file 
to expose the endpoint over HTTP:propertiesmanagement.endpoints.web.exposure.include=loggers
Use code with caution.

Step B: View or Change Levels via HTTP RequestsTo View All Loggers (GET):bashcurl -X GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/loggers
Use code with caution.

To Change a Specific Log Level (POST):Send a POST request to /actuator/loggers/{package}. 
The configuredLevel must be provided in a JSON body (e.g., DEBUG, INFO).
To Revert to Default (Reset):Pass null as the configuredLevel to remove custom configuration and inherit the root level.

2. How to Dynamically Change Configuration PropertiesTo reload properties (e.g., feature toggles, database settings) 
without a restart, use Spring Cloud and the /actuator/refresh endpoint.

Step A: Add DependenciesInclude the Spring Cloud context starter in your pom.xml:xml<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter</artifactId>
</dependency>
Use code with caution.
Step B: Enable the Refresh EndpointExpose the endpoint in your application.properties:
propertiesmanagement.endpoints.web.exposure.include=refresh,loggers
Use code with caution.

Step C: Annotate Beans with @RefreshScopeAnnotate Spring components (using @Value or @ConfigurationProperties) with 
@RefreshScope to allow re-initialization upon configuration changes.

Step D: Trigger the Configuration ReloadUpdate the external configuration file.
POST to the refresh endpoint: 
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/actuator/refresh.

The application will update the Environment and recreate annotated beans instantly.

⚠️ Security Production Warning: Endpoints like /actuator/loggers or /actuator/refresh should be protected 
by Spring Security to prevent unauthorized access.
